[{:db/id -1
  :source/dataset "kip"
  :kip/id "kip-0001"
  :kip/number 1
  :kip/title "Normative surfaces change only through a Final KIP"
  :kip/track :standards
  :kip/status :last-call
  :kip/author "root@junkawasaki.com"
  :kip/created "2026-08-16"
  :kip/last-call-started "2026-08-16"
  :kip/superseded-by nil

  :kip/abstract
  "Adopt the eight paths currently listed as :proposed in
   lang/normative-surfaces.edn, and make a change to any adopted surface
   require a Final KIP that names it. Ships the coverage check that decides
   whether that happened, and makes the check say 'could not answer' out loud
   instead of passing when it cannot see its inputs."

  :kip/motivation
  "KIP-0000 gives the workspace a queue. A queue nothing is required to enter is
   a suggestion.

   Seven paths are already normative because docs/authority-map.edn says so, and
   they are transcribed into :adopted. Eight more behave exactly like normative
   surfaces and are not named anywhere as such:

     lang/version-policy.edn            the 180-day deprecation floor lives here
     lang/capability-semantics.edn      the core capability IR (ADR-2608160200)
     lang/value-codec.edn               canonical encoding, therefore CIDs
     lang/elaboration-pipeline.edn      already gated on by docs/lang/gates.md
     lang/conformance/manifest.edn      what a conforming implementation runs
     lang/capability-conformance/manifest.edn
     lang/type-conformance/manifest.edn
     lang/kip-process.edn               this process, governing itself

   The first of those is the sharpest case. version-policy.edn is where the
   promise 'we will not remove a deprecated language profile for at least 180
   days' is written down. A silent edit shortens a promise already made to
   everyone who wrote code against profile 5. Nothing today would notice."

  :kip/specification
  "1. Every entry currently under :proposed in lang/normative-surfaces.edn moves
      to :adopted. authority-map.edn in kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang gains a
      :change-process authority pointing at this repository, so the two
      declarations agree and neither becomes the shadow of the other.

   2. A change to an adopted path requires a Final KIP whose :kip/surfaces names
      that surface's :id. The KIP must be Final *before* the change lands, which
      is the only ordering under which the objection window means anything.

   3. Coverage is checked by scripts/verify-kip-coverage.cljs in the
      superproject, which needs both the sibling checkouts and this registry.
      Its exit codes are 0 pass, 1 fail, 2 could-not-answer. It prints
      SCANNED<TAB>n and refuses to report a pass when n is 0.

      It is not a fleet gate, and adding it as one would repeat a mistake this
      workspace has already made twice. A fleet gate is handed one repository's
      tree; root-permit-index reads orgs/cloud-itonami, which git ls-files in
      the root repository does not contain, and it has been red about 300 times
      without once being answerable.

   4. Exemption: a change that only corrects a typo, a comment, or formatting,
      and leaves the parsed value byte-identical, does not need a KIP. The
      coverage check decides this by comparing parsed values, not text, so the
      exemption cannot be claimed by asserting it.

   5. This KIP does not apply retroactively. Every existing state of every
      adopted surface is grandfathered as of the commit this KIP reaches Final
      on. Retroactive application would require reconstructing a proposal for
      several hundred past commits, which produces paperwork and no objection
      window, because the window closed years ago."

  :kip/surfaces
  "[:version-policy :capability-semantics :value-codec :elaboration-pipeline
    :conformance-manifest :capability-conformance-manifest
    :type-conformance-manifest :kip-process]"
  :kip/requires "[\"kip-0000\"]"
  :kip/evidence
  "[\"all 16 paths in lang/normative-surfaces.edn verified present 2026-08-16\"
    \"docs/lang/gates.md already runs `test -f` on elaboration-pipeline.edn and the three conformance manifests\"
    \"lang/version-policy.edn :deprecation {:minimum-days 180 :removal-requires-major true} — the promise this protects\"]"
  :kip/migration
  "No existing file changes content. Authors of changes to the eight newly
   adopted paths must open a KIP first; before this is Final, nothing is
   enforced. Grandfathering (point 5) means no historical commit becomes
   non-conforming."
